    "description": "Technology education in Iran includes the development of digital competencies, university computing instruction, and vocational preparation for technical work. Available sources document policy initiatives, advanced university courses, and instructor-training reform, while leaving substantial gaps in comparable enrollment, learning-outcome, and employment data.",
    "tags": ["Technology education", "Digital skills", "Programming", "Artificial intelligence", "Vocational education", "Teacher training"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:37:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:37:29Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Technology Education in Iran: Skills, Computing and Training | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's technology-learning landscape through digital-skills policy, university AI courses, and vocational instructor training."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope-and-distinctions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Learning technology and learning through technology",
        "content": "Technology education involves more than putting lessons online. Learning to program, understand an algorithm, or manage information concerns the knowledge being taught. A digital classroom concerns the means of teaching it. The two can overlap, but evidence that a platform exists does not establish that its users have acquired technical competence.\n\nUNESCO's Iran technology profile documents policies addressing both sides of this distinction: electronic educational provision and the development of computational thinking and online literacy. Computational thinking means approaching problems in ways that can be expressed as systematic procedures; it is broader than familiarity with a particular application. The profile records a policy framework rather than a national assessment of what learners can actually do. [s1](https://gem-report-education-profiles.unesco.org/central-and-southern-asia/iran-islamic-republic-of/_technology.html)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "digital-competencies",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Digital competencies in general education",
        "content": "UNESCO identifies a 2022 resolution requiring the Ministry of Education to develop guidance for computational-thinking curricula and cyberspace literacy. Its account also describes policies for educational platforms and the Shad student network, including virtual classes and the development of digital literacy among teachers and students. These measures concern the foundations on which more specialized learning may build. [s1](https://gem-report-education-profiles.unesco.org/central-and-southern-asia/iran-islamic-republic-of/_technology.html)\n\nThe profile reports that it found no information on digital skills in initial or in-service teacher training. That is a limitation of the evidence assembled for the profile, not proof that no such training existed. The page is now archived, so its descriptions should be read with their historical context rather than as a complete statement of current implementation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "university-computing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From programming foundations to artificial intelligence",
        "content": "A publicly accessible artificial-intelligence course at Sharif University of Technology shows how advanced technical study combines programming with mathematical foundations. Its stated prerequisites include Python, data structures and algorithms, and knowledge of probability and linear algebra. Python is a programming language; data structures and algorithms concern how information is organized and how computational tasks are performed. Probability supports reasoning under uncertainty, while linear algebra supplies tools for working with vectors and matrices. [s2](https://sut-ai.github.io/)\n\nThe published syllabus covers search, constraint satisfaction, decision-making, probabilistic inference, machine learning, and neural networks. This breadth matters: artificial intelligence is presented as a collection of methods for representing problems and constructing solutions, rather than simply using a conversational application. The course also describes homework, quizzes, and examinations, providing evidence of an assessed academic course. Its stated learning objectives are intended outcomes, however, not independently measured attainment. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "specialization-and-responsibility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Specialization includes responsible use",
        "content": "Sharif's separate large-language-model course describes instruction in model architecture, training, fine-tuning, and applications. Large language models are computational systems trained to process and generate language. The course description also includes bias mitigation and fairness, placing questions about responsible deployment alongside technical methods. [s3](https://sut-llm.github.io/)\n\nThis is evidence of a specialized course, not evidence that the same curriculum is available throughout Iran. Nor does public access to a course website establish open enrollment, a free qualification, or recognition by employers. Readers should distinguish a published syllabus from an admissions offer and distinguish the instructor's educational aims from demonstrated graduate outcomes. The example nevertheless shows how an advanced subject can connect technical construction with scrutiny of how a system behaves and whom it may disadvantage. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "vocational-pathways",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Vocational training and the preparation of instructors",
        "content": "University courses are only one part of the landscape. UNESCO describes a project to strengthen Iran's Instructor Training Center in Karaj, implemented with UNESCO-UNEVOC, the Technical and Vocational Training Organization, and support from the Korea International Cooperation Agency. Its stated training areas include information and communication technology, instructional technology and multimedia, and pedagogy, entrepreneurship, and interpersonal skills. The project is scheduled across 2023–2028. [s4](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/transforming-vocational-education-iran-innovating-irans-instructor-training-centre?hub=370)\n\nThe emphasis on instructors is significant: curriculum relevance also depends on how trainers explain concepts, organize practical work, and assess learners. UNESCO describes equipment and training reform as project intentions; the announcement does not establish nationwide improvements in completion or employment. [s4]",
