        "id": "reading-the-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence does and does not show",
        "content": "These sources establish distinct layers: policies for broad digital competencies, assessed university instruction, specialized study, and vocational instructor development. They do not provide a comparable national account of participation or effectiveness. A strong overview must also examine cybersecurity and data-science provision, basic digital inclusion, and short commercial training programs before drawing conclusions about the whole sector.\n\nThe central editorial distinction is between provision and results. A policy documents an intention, a syllabus documents planned instruction, and a project announcement documents a proposed intervention. None alone measures what learners retained, whether opportunities were distributed evenly, or how training affected employment. Those questions require additional evidence.",
